AI - Generated Paper

A Taxonomy of Hallucination Types in
Large Language Models for Research
Applications
Abstract
Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used across research workflows for literature
discovery, synthesis, question answering, coding, data interpretation, summarization, drafting,
and citation generation. Their usefulness is constrained, however, by hallucinations: outputs that
are fluent and plausible but false, unsupported, internally inconsistent, or unfaithful to an
information source or user-provided context. Existing literature has established several related
distinctions, including intrinsic versus extrinsic hallucination, factuality versus faithfulness
hallucination, and errors arising from model knowledge versus failures of source grounding. Yet
these classifications do not fully capture the heterogeneous failure modes encountered in
research applications, where fabricated citations, incorrect quantitative claims, temporal
knowledge errors, unsupported synthesis, methodological misrepresentation, and erroneous
tool-mediated evidence can have consequences beyond ordinary question-answering errors.
This paper develops a research-oriented taxonomy that organizes hallucinations along three
complementary dimensions: epistemic relation, failure locus, and research manifestation.
The resulting framework distinguishes factual hallucinations, source-faithfulness hallucinations,
reasoning and inferential hallucinations, citation and bibliographic hallucinations, methodological
hallucinations, temporal and version hallucinations, and tool- or retrieval-mediated
hallucinations. The paper reviews established detection and mitigation approaches, including
factuality metrics, retrieval-augmented generation, self-verification, semantic uncertainty, and
citation-aware generation. It argues that no single evaluation paradigm is adequate because
hallucination detection is task-, domain-, evidence-, and granularity-dependent. Finally, the
paper identifies research gaps concerning benchmark realism, uncertainty calibration,
provenance, longitudinal knowledge change, domain-specific evaluation, causal diagnosis, and
human–LLM verification. A research-oriented taxonomy can therefore serve not merely as a
descriptive classification but as a basis for choosing evaluation protocols, designing safeguards,
and specifying trustworthy uses of LLMs in scholarly research.
Keywords: large language models; hallucination; factuality; faithfulness; research integrity;
citation hallucination; retrieval-augmented generation; uncertainty estimation; provenance;
scholarly AI

1. Introduction
Large Language Models have transformed natural-language processing from a collection of
narrowly specified prediction tasks into general-purpose systems capable of answering
questions, summarizing documents, generating explanations, writing code, and interacting with
external tools. These capabilities have rapidly expanded their use in scholarly and professional
research workflows. Researchers increasingly employ LLMs for literature exploration,
summarization, drafting, data extraction, methodological assistance, hypothesis generation, and
interaction with scientific databases. At the same time, the models remain probabilistic
generators rather than databases of verified propositions. Consequently, fluent language can
coexist with inaccurate or unsupported claims.
The literature commonly uses the term hallucination for generated content that is factually
incorrect, unverifiable, or inconsistent with a source or instruction (Ji et al., 2023; Huang et al.,
2024). Earlier work on neural text generation emphasized hallucination in task-specific settings
such as summarization and data-to-text generation, where the central issue was whether
generated text was supported by an input source (Maynez et al., 2020). With the emergence of
open-ended LLMs, the problem has broadened. An LLM may produce a false statement even
when no explicit source is provided, contradict an authoritative source supplied in the prompt,
fabricate a publication that appears bibliographically plausible, misattribute a finding to an
authentic article, or combine individually plausible observations into an unsupported scientific
conclusion.
This diversity presents a taxonomy problem. A classification designed for abstractive
summarization does not adequately distinguish a fabricated DOI from an incorrect interpretation
of an authentic paper. Conversely, a simple factual/non-factual distinction is insufficient for
applications in which the relevant question is not only whether a statement is true, but whether it
follows from a cited source, whether the model has accurately represented a methodology, or
whether the evidence was available at the claimed date.
Two broad conceptual distinctions are especially influential. The first is factuality, concerning
agreement between generated claims and external reality or verifiable evidence. The second is
faithfulness, concerning whether an output appropriately follows a supplied source, context, or
instruction (Huang et al., 2024). Earlier summarization research similarly distinguished intrinsic
errors, which contradict information in a source, from extrinsic errors, which introduce
information not supported by that source (Maynez et al., 2020). These distinctions remain
useful, but research applications require additional categories because the scholarly object
being represented may itself be a paper, citation, dataset, experiment, result, method, or
historical state of knowledge.
This paper therefore proposes a taxonomy specifically oriented toward research applications. Its
central argument is that hallucination should be represented as a multidimensional
phenomenon rather than a single error class. In particular, the paper distinguishes: (1) the
relation between a claim and the relevant truth or evidence, (2) the stage or mechanism at
which the failure arises, and (3) the concrete research artifact or activity affected by the failure.

This perspective is important because mitigation strategies are not interchangeable. Retrieval
can reduce unsupported factual claims but cannot guarantee correct interpretation of a retrieved
paper. Self-consistency can identify some uncertainty but cannot establish bibliographic
existence. Citation verification can detect fabricated references but does not ensure that an
authentic citation actually supports the associated statement. A robust research assistant
therefore requires a layered architecture of retrieval, provenance, verification, uncertainty
estimation, and human oversight.

2. Background and Related Work
2.1 From neural-generation hallucination to LLM hallucination
Hallucination predates contemporary LLMs. In neural machine translation and abstractive
summarization, generated text could be fluent while introducing entities, facts, or relationships
absent from the source. Maynez et al. (2020) demonstrated that abstractive summarization
systems can produce substantial amounts of unfaithful content and that standard lexical metrics
such as ROUGE are insufficient for reliably measuring factual faithfulness. This work helped
establish textual entailment and source-grounded evaluation as alternatives to surface-form
overlap.
Kryscinski et al. (2020) introduced FactCC, a factual-consistency model trained to identify
whether summary sentences are supported by their source documents. Goyal and Durrett
(2020) further proposed dependency-level entailment to localize factual inconsistencies rather
than reducing an entire output to a sentence-level judgment. Other approaches employed
question generation and question answering to compare what a source and summary imply
about the same entities or claims.
These developments established a foundational insight: fluency is not evidence of factuality.
A model can produce highly coherent language while failing to preserve the truth conditions of
the source.
2.2 Taxonomies of LLM hallucination
Ji et al. (2023) provided a broad survey of hallucination in natural language generation and
synthesized distinctions across translation, dialogue, summarization, and other tasks. More
recent surveys have adapted the taxonomy to general-purpose LLMs. Huang et al. (2024), for
example, distinguish factuality hallucination from faithfulness hallucination and organize
hallucination causes around data, training, and inference factors. Their framework highlights the
fact that hallucinations can originate from limitations in training data and memorized knowledge,
alignment objectives, prompting, decoding, or contextual grounding.

The distinction is valuable but does not by itself specify what kind of research activity has failed.
A statement such as "Smith et al. (2021) reported a significant effect" can be wrong because the
paper does not exist, because the paper exists but did not report the effect, because the result
was associated with a different study, or because the underlying effect was statistically
misunderstood. All four cases may be classified as factual or faithfulness failures, but their
detection and remediation requirements differ substantially.
2.3 Factuality and truthfulness
Truthfulness benchmarks such as TruthfulQA examine whether language models repeat
common falsehoods and misconceptions learned from human text (Lin et al., 2022). Such work
illustrates an important mechanism of hallucination: a model can reproduce plausible linguistic
patterns associated with false propositions even when the propositions themselves are
incorrect. In TruthfulQA, model scale did not automatically guarantee truthfulness; some larger
systems performed worse on the benchmark than smaller models.
FActScore introduced a more fine-grained approach for long-form generation by decomposing
text into atomic facts and estimating what proportion of those facts are supported by a reliable
knowledge source (Min et al., 2023). This approach is particularly relevant to research writing
because an apparently reliable paragraph can contain a mixture of correct, partially correct, and
fabricated claims. Evaluating at the atomic-claim level therefore provides more diagnostic
information than assigning one binary factuality label to an entire response.
2.4 Detecting hallucinations through uncertainty and consistency
Another research direction asks whether the model itself contains signals indicating when it is
likely to be wrong. Kadavath et al. (2022) studied whether language models can assess the
validity of their own claims and introduced probability-based measures such as "P(True)" and
"P(IK)" ("I know"). Their findings suggested that models can possess useful but imperfect
information about their own uncertainty.
Farquhar et al. (2024) developed semantic entropy, which estimates uncertainty at the level of
meaning rather than surface wording. Their results showed that semantic diversity among
sampled answers can provide a signal for hallucination, particularly for questions where the
model lacks reliable knowledge. Such approaches shift hallucination detection from post hoc
fact checking toward uncertainty-aware generation and selective prediction.
Manakul et al. (2023) introduced SelfCheckGPT, a black-box method that samples multiple
responses and identifies likely hallucinations when supposedly factual claims vary substantially
across samples. The method illustrates another important principle: inconsistency across
semantically equivalent generations can be evidence of epistemic uncertainty, although
consistency alone cannot establish truth.

3. A Research-Oriented Taxonomy of LLM Hallucinations
3.1 Taxonomy design principles
For research applications, a useful taxonomy should satisfy four criteria. First, categories should
be operationally distinguishable, so that different classes can be evaluated with different
methods. Second, the taxonomy should accommodate both open-domain factual claims and
source-grounded research tasks. Third, it should distinguish errors in content from errors in
evidence representation. Fourth, it should support mitigation mapping, allowing researchers to
identify which controls can address a particular failure.
The proposed framework uses three complementary dimensions:
1. Epistemic relation: What is the relationship between the output and the truth/evidence?
2. Failure locus: At what point in the generation pipeline does the error arise?
3. Research manifestation: What research artifact or activity does the error affect?
The categories below are therefore not necessarily mutually exclusive. A single output may
simultaneously contain a source-faithfulness hallucination and a citation hallucination.
3.2 Type I: Factual or world-knowledge hallucination
A factual hallucination is a claim that conflicts with verifiable external knowledge or lacks
adequate factual support when presented as fact.
Examples include incorrect dates, invented people, wrong numerical values, false historical
statements, or misidentified scientific findings. This is the most familiar form of hallucination and
corresponds closely to factuality-oriented definitions in recent surveys (Ji et al., 2023; Huang et
al., 2024).
Within research contexts, factual hallucinations are especially consequential when they
concern:
● numerical results;
● sample sizes;
● experimental outcomes;
● statistical significance;
● dataset properties;
● author names;
● publication dates;
● institutional affiliations;
● scientific definitions; or
● technical specifications.

Factual errors may be atomic, affecting one proposition, or compositional, where several small
inaccuracies accumulate into a misleading explanation. FActScore's atomic decomposition is
particularly relevant because research prose often contains many independently checkable
propositions (Min et al., 2023).
3.3 Type II: Source-faithfulness hallucination
A source-faithfulness hallucination occurs when generated content is inconsistent with,
unsupported by, or improperly derived from an identified source.
This category is central to literature reviews, document summarization, systematic reviews, and
evidence synthesis. A source may state that an intervention was associated with an outcome
while the LLM summarizes it as demonstrating causation. Similarly, a paper may report a result
only for one subgroup while the model generalizes it to an entire population.
The distinction from ordinary factual hallucination is important. A generated statement can even
be factually true in the real world while being unfaithful to the cited source. For example, a
claim may be scientifically established elsewhere but not actually reported by the article being
summarized.
The intrinsic/extrinsic distinction from summarization research remains useful here. Intrinsic
errors contradict source information, whereas extrinsic errors introduce unsupported information
(Maynez et al., 2020).
3.4 Type III: Citation and bibliographic hallucination
A citation hallucination involves fabricated, malformed, or improperly attributed scholarly
references. It includes at least four subtypes:
1. Existence hallucination: the cited paper, book, dataset, or DOI does not exist.
2. Metadata hallucination: the work exists, but authors, title, venue, date, pages, or
identifier are incorrect.
3. Attribution hallucination: a real work is cited for a claim it does not support.
4. Citation-context hallucination: an authentic reference is inserted into a paragraph in a
way that falsely suggests evidentiary support.
This category deserves explicit recognition in research-oriented taxonomies because
bibliographic references are not merely prose decorations; they are part of the evidentiary
infrastructure of scholarship.
Empirical studies demonstrate the seriousness of the problem. Walters and Wilder (2023) found
that, across their evaluated literature-review prompts, fabricated references were common,
although GPT-4 substantially reduced fabrication relative to GPT-3.5. Gravel et al. (2023)
similarly reported a high proportion of fabricated references in answers to medical questions.

These findings demonstrate that citation hallucination can remain present even when the
generated prose appears professionally written.
A useful extension is identifier hallucination, in which a model supplies a plausible but
nonexistent DOI, PMID, ISBN, arXiv identifier, or URL. Identifier verification is therefore an
important component of scholarly LLM evaluation.
3.5 Type IV: Reasoning and inferential hallucination
A reasoning hallucination occurs when the model derives an unsupported conclusion from
otherwise plausible premises. Unlike a simple factual error, the individual statements may be
accurate while the inference connecting them is invalid.
Examples include:
● confusing correlation with causation;
● inferring statistical significance from a large effect size without considering uncertainty;
● treating absence of evidence as evidence of absence;
● inferring generalizability from a narrow sample;
● converting an observational finding into an experimental conclusion;
● claiming that two studies are contradictory when they use different populations or
measures.
This category is especially important for research assistance because LLMs are frequently
asked not only to summarize findings but to compare studies, identify causes, assess evidence
quality, and generate hypotheses.
Reasoning hallucinations challenge simple fact-checking because verifying individual
propositions may not reveal the inferential failure. Evaluation must therefore sometimes operate
on argument structure, assumptions, and logical entailment.
3.6 Type V: Methodological hallucination
A methodological hallucination occurs when an LLM incorrectly represents a research
method, experimental procedure, dataset construction process, statistical technique, or
analytical protocol.
Examples include attributing a randomized controlled trial design to an observational study,
stating that a paper used logistic regression when it used a survival model, or claiming that a
dataset contains variables that are absent from its published documentation.
Methodological hallucination deserves a separate class because methods are often used as the
basis for judging evidence quality. An incorrect representation can therefore distort not only one
fact but the researcher's entire assessment of a study.

A related problem is procedural invention, where the model fills missing experimental details
with plausible-sounding assumptions. Such details may be particularly difficult to identify
because the invented method can be scientifically reasonable even though it was never
performed.
3.7 Type VI: Temporal, version, and knowledge-boundary hallucination
A temporal hallucination arises when a model incorrectly represents the state of knowledge at
a particular time. Examples include citing a result as available before its publication, treating a
draft or preprint as a peer-reviewed article, applying a current standard to a historical study, or
describing a software library's current behavior as though it existed in an earlier version.
This category follows from the fact that research knowledge is dynamic. A statement can be true
in 2026 and false as a description of the scientific record in 2021.
Temporal hallucination should therefore be distinguished from ordinary factual error. The central
question is not simply "Is this statement true?" but "Was this statement true, established, or
knowable within the specified temporal frame?"
3.8 Type VII: Retrieval- and tool-mediated hallucination
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) can reduce reliance on parametric knowledge by
supplying external evidence (Lewis et al., 2020), but retrieval does not eliminate hallucination.
Instead, it introduces new error pathways:
● retrieving an irrelevant document;
● retrieving outdated information;
● retrieving a secondary source when a primary source is required;
● misreading retrieved evidence;
● ignoring contradictory passages;
● attributing information to the wrong document; or
● generating claims not supported by retrieved text.
Thus a model can hallucinate because retrieval failed or despite successful retrieval.
RAGAS explicitly treats retrieval relevance, faithfulness to retrieved context, and generation
quality as distinct evaluation dimensions (Es et al., 2024).
Corrective Retrieval-Augmented Generation (CRAG) and Self-RAG illustrate attempts to
address these weaknesses. CRAG evaluates retrieved material and triggers corrective retrieval
when necessary, while Self-RAG trains the model to retrieve, generate, and critique selectively
(Yan et al., 2024; Asai et al., 2024).
3.9 Type VIII: Self-verification and tool-use hallucination

A newer class concerns hallucinations about the model's own actions. An LLM may claim that
it searched a database, opened a paper, executed code, checked a DOI, or verified a result
when it did not actually perform that operation.
This is conceptually distinct from ordinary factual hallucination because the false proposition
concerns process provenance. Research environments increasingly involve tool-augmented
agents, making this category increasingly relevant.
A trustworthy research system must therefore distinguish between:
● evidence actually retrieved;
● evidence supplied by the user;
● information inferred from model parameters; and
● verification steps that were actually executed.
A model should not be allowed to convert an intended action into a claimed completed action.
3.10 Type IX: Synthesis and attribution hallucination
Research commonly involves integrating multiple papers rather than summarizing one source. A
synthesis hallucination occurs when the model constructs a cross-paper conclusion that is not
justified by the collection of sources.
For example, a model may correctly report results from Studies A, B, and C but then assert that
"the literature consistently demonstrates X" even though the studies use incompatible definitions
or reach divergent conclusions.
This category is distinct from simple citation misattribution because all cited papers may be
genuine and correctly represented individually. The hallucination occurs at the level of
synthesis.

4. Causes and Mechanisms
Hallucinations arise from interacting properties of data, learning objectives, model architecture,
inference, and task design (Ji et al., 2023; Huang et al., 2024).
4.1 Imperfect and heterogeneous training data
Web-scale corpora contain misinformation, contradictions, duplicated material, outdated
information, low-quality sources, and incomplete documentation. A language model trained to
predict text is therefore not directly optimized to distinguish reliable knowledge from unreliable
text.

TruthfulQA demonstrates one consequence: models may reproduce common misconceptions
because those misconceptions are represented in their training distribution (Lin et al., 2022).
4.2 Parametric knowledge and incomplete retrieval
LLMs encode information indirectly in parameters. This knowledge can be useful but may be
incomplete, outdated, entangled across contexts, or difficult to access precisely.
Knowledge-intensive tasks motivated the development of RAG precisely because parametric
models have limitations in accessing and updating factual information (Lewis et al., 2020).
4.3 Objective mismatch
The training objective for a conventional autoregressive model rewards likely continuations
rather than truth. Fluent continuation therefore does not necessarily imply factual verification.
This mismatch also explains why increasing model size alone cannot be assumed to solve
hallucination. Larger models may improve many capabilities while retaining systematic
weaknesses in truthfulness and calibration.
4.4 Instruction following and alignment pressure
Alignment methods encourage models to satisfy user requests and produce helpful responses.
In some circumstances, however, a strong pressure to answer can compete with the safer
behavior of explicitly stating uncertainty or refusing to speculate.
Research prompts frequently contain implicit expectations such as "provide the references,"
"explain why," or "identify the studies." If the model does not possess sufficient information, the
objective of being helpful can produce plausible completion rather than abstention.
4.5 Decoding and stochasticity
Sampling-based generation permits multiple plausible continuations. SelfCheckGPT exploits
this property by treating substantial disagreement among sampled outputs as a possible signal
of hallucination (Manakul et al., 2023). More generally, generation settings can influence factual
consistency, although decoding changes alone cannot solve underlying knowledge or grounding
problems.

5. Current Approaches to Detection and Mitigation
5.1 Human verification

Human expert review remains the strongest general-purpose safeguard for high-stakes
research claims, but it is expensive and difficult to scale. Human reviewers also exhibit domain
and source-selection biases. A practical system therefore needs automated screening to
prioritize human attention.
5.2 Claim-level factuality evaluation
FActScore represents an important shift toward atomic claim evaluation. Rather than asking
whether an entire response is factual, the evaluation estimates the fraction of atomic statements
that are independently supported (Min et al., 2023).
This approach is particularly appropriate for research assistants because a single paragraph
can contain multiple claims about authors, methods, datasets, results, and interpretations.
5.3 Entailment- and QA-based evaluation
FactCC, dependency-level entailment, and question-answering approaches evaluate whether a
generated statement follows from a reference document (Kryscinski et al., 2020; Goyal &
Durrett, 2020). These methods are useful for summarization and source-grounded generation,
but their performance can degrade when claims are complex, implicit, multi-document, or highly
specialized.
5.4 Sampling-based self-consistency
SelfCheckGPT uses multiple generations to identify information that is unstable across samples
(Manakul et al., 2023). This is attractive for black-box systems because it does not require
access to model probabilities or an external knowledge base.
However, self-consistency should not be confused with correctness. A model may repeatedly
produce the same false statement because that statement is strongly encoded in its
parameters.
5.5 Semantic uncertainty
Semantic entropy is designed to aggregate uncertainty over meanings rather than token
sequences. Farquhar et al. (2024) showed that semantic entropy can detect an important class
of hallucinations associated with lack of model knowledge.
Its research value lies in supporting selective answering. Instead of requiring the system to
answer every question, the model can abstain or trigger external verification when semantic
uncertainty is high.
5.6 Retrieval-Augmented Generation

RAG connects a generator to an external corpus, allowing the system to use information beyond
its parametric memory (Lewis et al., 2020). For research applications, this can facilitate access
to current literature and provide source provenance.
Yet RAG must not be treated as synonymous with factuality. Retrieval introduces errors of
relevance, coverage, freshness, ranking, and interpretation. A retrieved passage can itself be
low quality or contradictory.
RAGAS explicitly decomposes evaluation into retrieval quality, context utilization/faithfulness,
and generation quality (Es et al., 2024). Self-RAG further allows a model to decide when
retrieval is needed and to critique its own evidence and generation (Asai et al., 2024). CRAG
introduces a retrieval-quality evaluator and corrective retrieval procedures (Yan et al., 2024).
5.7 Chain-of-Verification and post-generation verification
Chain-of-Verification (CoVe) first drafts a response, generates verification questions, answers
those questions independently, and then constructs a revised answer (Dhuliawala et al., 2023).
This architecture addresses an important failure mode: a single generation may reinforce its
own unsupported claims.
Verification, however, remains vulnerable when the model is both generator and verifier.
Independent retrieval, external databases, symbolic tools, or human review can provide
stronger epistemic independence.
5.8 Citation verification
Research-oriented LLMs should treat references as structured data requiring verification rather
than as unconstrained text generation. A reliable pipeline should validate, when possible:
● publication existence;
● author–title consistency;
● venue and year;
● DOI or other identifier;
● bibliographic metadata; and
● whether the source actually supports the cited claim.
Evidence from fabricated-citation studies demonstrates why this layer is necessary (Walters &
Wilder, 2023; Gravel et al., 2023).

