7. Research Challenges and Limitations
7.1 No universally accepted operational definition
Hallucination remains a broad term covering several conceptually different phenomena. A model
output may be false, unverifiable, unsupported by a provided source, logically invalid, or merely
uncertain. Treating all such cases as equivalent complicates benchmarking and makes results
difficult to compare across studies.
A robust evaluation protocol should therefore declare the reference standard: world
knowledge, source document, database record, scientific consensus, or a specified temporal
snapshot.
7.2 Evaluation depends on granularity
Whole-response evaluation can hide substantial local errors. Conversely, atomic-claim
evaluation may fragment complex methodological or inferential statements into pieces that
appear individually correct despite a flawed overall argument.
Future benchmarks should therefore operate at multiple granularities:
1. token/entity level;
2. atomic claim level;
3. sentence level;
4. argument level;
5. source/document level; and
6. research-conclusion level.
7.3 Automatic evaluators can hallucinate
An important limitation is evaluator reliability. LLM-based judges may themselves produce
incorrect judgments, creating an evaluator–generator dependency. Even entailment models can

fail when claims involve specialized terminology, numerical reasoning, negation, or implicit
relationships.
Accordingly, evaluation systems should report agreement with expert human judgments and test
the robustness of automatic metrics rather than treating model-generated scores as ground
truth.
7.4 Domain expertise remains indispensable
Scientific and technical hallucinations often involve subtle distinctions that general-purpose
evaluators cannot reliably recognize. Whether an inference is justified may depend on domain
conventions, statistical assumptions, experimental design, or specialized vocabulary.
A generic hallucination detector may therefore perform well on Wikipedia-style questions while
failing on specialized biomedical, legal, engineering, or social-science content.
7.5 Bibliographic verification is not enough
A reference can be authentic while the associated claim is unsupported. Therefore,
bibliographic validation should be viewed as only one stage in a citation-verification pipeline.
Research systems should distinguish at least:
source existence → metadata correctness → source accessibility → claim support →
interpretation correctness.
7.6 RAG creates a false sense of security
RAG improves access to external information but can introduce retrieval-specific failure modes.
Poor retrieval, incomplete evidence, context overload, or incorrect interpretation can still
produce hallucinated conclusions. RAG systems should therefore expose retrieval provenance
and evidence coverage rather than merely displaying a list of sources.
7.7 Temporal drift
Research information changes over time. Benchmarks that use static truth sets may become
outdated, especially for software, medical knowledge, policies, standards, and rapidly
developing scientific areas.
Future datasets should support versioned or date-indexed ground truth.

8. Research Gaps and Future Directions

8.1 Standardized multidimensional benchmarks
Current benchmarks frequently focus on one dimension—truthfulness, factual consistency, or
hallucination recognition. Future research should build multidimensional research
hallucination benchmarks in which each example is annotated for:
● factual correctness;
● source support;
● citation correctness;
● methodological accuracy;
● inferential validity;
● temporal validity;
● evidence provenance; and
● uncertainty/abstention appropriateness.
This would make it possible to compare systems across genuinely different failure modes.
8.2 Provenance-native language models
Research assistants should not merely produce answers; they should maintain explicit
provenance graphs connecting each claim to its evidence. A future response representation
could encode:
[
\text{Claim} \rightarrow \text{Evidence Passage} \rightarrow \text{Document} \rightarrow
\text{Identifier} \rightarrow \text{Verification State}.
]
Such representations could turn hallucination prevention from a purely linguistic problem into an
information-provenance problem.
8.3 Claim-level uncertainty and calibrated abstention
Future systems should estimate confidence at the level of individual claims rather than entire
responses. A response might contain ten claims where eight are highly supported and two
require verification.
This suggests an architecture in which the model can produce:
● supported;
● weakly supported;
● conflicting evidence;
● unverifiable; or
● high-uncertainty

labels alongside claims.
Semantic entropy and self-evaluation research provide promising foundations, but stronger
calibration and external validation are needed (Kadavath et al., 2022; Farquhar et al., 2024).
8.4 Temporal and version-aware research assistants
Scholarly systems should explicitly model time. Literature agents should distinguish a preprint
from its peer-reviewed version, identify retractions and corrections, and preserve the state of
evidence at the date relevant to the research question.
Temporal provenance is particularly important for systematic reviews and longitudinal scientific
analyses.
8.5 Citation verification as an integrated architecture
Citation checking should occur during generation rather than only after a paper has been
drafted. Future systems could retrieve candidate sources, verify bibliographic records, identify
supporting passages, and prohibit unsupported citations from appearing in the final manuscript.
The empirical evidence on fabricated citations strongly motivates this direction (Walters &
Wilder, 2023). More recent scholarship on reference hallucinations further suggests that citation
constraints and task structure can materially influence the form of hallucination produced by
LLMs (Davis & Mahmoud, 2026).
8.6 Independent verification agents
A generator should not necessarily be its own final verifier. Future architectures could employ
specialized independent agents:
● a retrieval agent for evidence discovery;
● a bibliographic agent for metadata verification;
● a factuality agent for claim checking;
● a reasoning agent for inferential validity;
● a methodology agent for experimental-design interpretation; and
● a provenance controller to ensure that claims are traceable.
Such modular verification could be more reliable than asking a single general-purpose model to
"check its work."
8.7 Researcher-centered human–AI interaction
The goal should not necessarily be zero hallucination, which may be unrealistic for open-ended
generation. A more practical target is risk-aware assistance. Systems should surface uncertain

claims, contradictory evidence, missing citations, and unverifiable statements instead of silently
filling gaps.
In scholarly settings, an LLM that occasionally abstains transparently may be more useful than
one that generates polished but unverified prose.
8.8 Evaluating downstream research harm
Future work should move beyond measuring hallucination rates and investigate consequences.
A fabricated citation in a casual brainstorming session may be inconvenient; the same error in a
systematic review, clinical guideline, or meta-analysis can contaminate downstream evidence.
Benchmarks should therefore evaluate not only frequency, but severity, detectability,
propagation, and downstream impact.

9. Conclusion
Hallucination in Large Language Models is not a single failure mode. For research applications,
it is a family of failures involving truth, evidence, inference, provenance, methodology, time,
retrieval, and scholarly attribution. Existing distinctions such as intrinsic versus extrinsic
hallucination and factuality versus faithfulness provide essential foundations, but they do not
fully capture the complexity of research-oriented use.
This paper proposed a multidimensional taxonomy comprising factual, source-faithfulness,
citation/bibliographic, reasoning, methodological, temporal/version, retrieval- and tool-mediated,
self-verification, and synthesis hallucinations. The taxonomy emphasizes that different
hallucination types require different forms of evidence and different mitigation strategies.
Current approaches—including FActScore, SelfCheckGPT, semantic entropy, RAG, Self-RAG,
CRAG, and Chain-of-Verification—demonstrate substantial progress. Nevertheless, their
effectiveness is conditional on task formulation, reference quality, domain expertise, and
evaluator reliability. No existing method guarantees that an LLM-generated research output is
simultaneously factually correct, source-faithful, methodologically accurate, logically valid,
temporally appropriate, and bibliographically authentic.
The central implication for research applications is therefore architectural rather than merely
model-centric: trustworthy LLM assistance requires claim-level evaluation, explicit
provenance, retrieval verification, citation checking, uncertainty estimation, temporal
awareness, and appropriately targeted human oversight. Future research should treat
hallucination taxonomy not simply as a catalog of mistakes but as a design framework linking
identifiable failure modes to evidence standards, evaluation procedures, and mitigation
mechanisms. Such a framework is necessary for moving LLMs from fluent research assistants
toward dependable components of scholarly knowledge production.
